        "op": "submit",
        "snippet": "/**\n * B121 entry validation: compose two independent spending instances in one Graph.\n *\n * The caller supplies the Graph and two independently prepared\n * SpendingCreationInputs values (each with its own input Nodes and its own\n * prepared resource). This module only composes; it never prepares inputs,\n * opens resources or invokes a transport.\n */\nimport type { Graph } from \"../../packages/ts/src/graph/graph.js\";\nimport { spendingAlertsFor, type SpendingCreationInputs } from \"./causal-entry.js\";\nimport { mountSpendingView, type SpendingViewObservation } from \"./causal-view-binding.js\";\n\nexport interface ComposedSpendingInstance {\n\treadonly name: string;\n\t/** The original composed instance; keep it with the owning Graph. */\n\treadonly app: ReturnType<ReturnType<typeof spendingAlertsFor>[\"compose\"]>;\n\t/** Ends observation only; run ownership stays with the application. */\n\treadonly detach: () => void;\n}\n\nexport interface TwoInstanceComposition {\n\treadonly first: ComposedSpendingInstance;\n\treadonly second: ComposedSpendingInstance;\n}\n\n/**\n * Compose two independent instances from one shared immutable default set.\n *\n * - `defaults` is bound once by `spendingAlertsFor`; both composes inherit it.\n * - Each instance gets its own name override, its own prepared inputs and its\n *   own resource binding; nothing is cloned or rebound here.\n * - Only the second instance enables summary diagnostics.\n * - Each instance is connected to its own render callback through the\n *   original `.view` object.\n */\nexport function composeTwoSpendingInstances(\n\tgraph: Graph,\n\tfirstInputs: SpendingCreationInputs,\n\tsecondInputs: SpendingCreationInputs,\n\trenderFirst: (observation: SpendingViewObservation) => void,\n\trenderSecond: (observation: SpendingViewObservation) => void,\n): TwoInstanceComposition {\n\t// One shared set of immutable defaults; configuration allocates no nodes.\n\tconst preset = spendingAlertsFor(graph, { name: \"alerts\", diagnostics: \"off\" });\n\n\t// First instance: distinct name, its own inputs/resource, diagnostics off.\n\tconst firstApp = preset.compose(firstInputs, { name: \"alerts-a\" });\n\n\t// Second instance: distinct name, its own inputs/resource, summary diagnostics.\n\tconst secondApp = preset.compose(secondInputs, {\n\t\tname: \"alerts-b\",\n\t\tdiagnostics: \"summary\",\n\t});\n\n\t// Each display observes only its own original view object.\n\tconst detachFirst = mountSpendingView(firstApp.view, renderFirst);\n\tconst detachSecond = mountSpendingView(secondApp.view, renderSecond);\n\n\treturn Object.freeze({\n\t\tfirst: Object.freeze({ name: \"alerts-a\", app: firstApp, detach: detachFirst }),\n\t\tsecond: Object.freeze({ name: \"alerts-b\", app: secondApp, detach: detachSecond }),\n\t});\n}\n",
        "explanation": "## What the function does\n\n`composeTwoSpendingInstances` takes the caller's `Graph` plus two independently prepared `SpendingCreationInputs` values and two render callbacks. It binds one shared immutable default set with `spendingAlertsFor(graph, { name: \"alerts\", diagnostics: \"off\" })` (causal-entry.ts:49-51), then calls `.compose(...)` twice on that same preset. Each compose invokes the existing complete host construction (causal-entry.ts:48-49, 59-69); configuration itself allocates no graph nodes (README.md:82-83).\n\n- First instance: `{ name: \"alerts-a\" }` — inherits `diagnostics: \"off\"` from the shared defaults (causal-entry.ts:39-40).\n- Second instance: `{ name: \"alerts-b\", diagnostics: \"summary\" }` — the only place summary diagnostics is enabled; `spendingNodeNames` adds `${name}/diagnosticSummary` only when diagnostics is `\"summary\"` (causal-preset.ts:60-72).\n\nEach instance is connected to its own render callback by passing its own original `.view` to `mountSpendingView` (causal-view-binding.ts:17-20); the returned function ends observation only (causal-view-binding.ts:16, 32-36). The function returns both instances and both cleanup functions. It does not open resources, invent input facts or invoke a transport; the resource is only claimed inside the host (causal-focused-host.ts:96, 110).\n\n## What the application must retain after either display is unmounted\n\nDetaching a panel stops observation only; the running instance retains outstanding obligations (README.md:143). The application must keep, per instance:\n\n1. **The owning Graph and the original composed instance together** (README.md:100-102). Keep `app.owner` and `app.inspect` (causal-graded-demo.ts:65); `inspect()` exposes `records` (request/admission/outcome), `inFlight`, `writes`, `fault`, `normalEndReady` (causal-focused-host.ts:558-576).\n2. **The retained request/admission/outcome records.** The host retains at most 64 distinct request records per owned epoch, including refusals, and one in-flight write; a busy/exhausted budget yields an exact cancelled/no-submit result with no queue or retry (README.md:146-149). Unknown or reconcile-required outcomes are not success and prevent normal-end readiness; the application must preserve those records instead of treating panel cleanup as recovery (README.md:149-151).\n3. **The original view and capability references** for reconnection and for frameworks: reconnecting reuses the original view (causal-graded-demo.ts:189, 297), and capabilities keep their original identity/execution/retained lineage (causal-graded-demo.ts:283-287; README.md:91-96).\n4. **The original input Nodes and the prepared resource binding**, because a name change does not clone input Nodes, create an independent resource or rewrite its binding (README.md:85-89).\n\n`runEndReady`/`inspect().normalEndReady` are current readiness conditions, not proof that lifecycle termination or resource release occurred (README.md:144); the protocol has no END message and business stop, node COMPLETE and run termination are distinct (README.md:153-157).\n\n## What the three distinctions contribute\n\n- **Distinct names** (`alerts-a` vs `alerts-b`) give each instance a distinct node namespace: `spendingNodeNames` prefixes every node with the instance name (causal-preset.ts:60-72), and the host adds `${name}/hostFacts`, `${name}/hostGuard`, `${name}/runEndReady`, `${name}/hostPackFacts` (causal-focused-host.ts:81-87). This keeps the two instances' nodes and evidence separable in one shared Graph. A name is not an authorization token (README.md:86).\n- **Independently prepared input Nodes** are the actual evidence lanes: `spendingInputNodes` requires six distinct input nodes across the four groups (causal-preset.ts:73-93), and the host consumes `inputs.evaluations.pack/arrivals/current`, `inputs.verification.receipts`, `inputs.localAuthority.facts` (causal-focused-host.ts:88-94). Distinct nodes keep each instance's occurrence, revision and provenance facts separate; the demo builds separate `input`/`input-b` sources (causal-graded-demo.ts:30-40, 95).\n- **Resource bindings** tie each instance to its own prepared resource: the host validates `same(resource.binding, binding)` and claims the resource for that epoch (causal-focused-host.ts:74-77, 96, 110). The binding carries `compositionEpoch`/`hostEpoch`/`runRef` (causal-inputs.ts:212-225), so the second instance uses a distinct binding/epoch (causal-graded-demo.ts:89-94) and its own resource (causal-graded-demo.ts:106-110). Sharing immutable defaults does not imply sharing a running instance or its resource (README.md:88-89).\n\nNo code was executed; this is a static composition over the supplied sources.",
